{
  "source": "2026-10-09-13Tool多场景联调/A-iam-pod-function-calling: first six actual queries + original report. Synthetic CoreDNS is separate.",
  "proposed": {
    "root_cause": "The iam-server pod cannot resolve its Redis dependency because the DNS name 'kylin-insights-redis' (and all of its search-suffix forms in the kylin-insights namespace) authoritatively returns NXDOMAIN from a healthy CoreDNS. This proves the Redis Service / DNS record does not exist in the cluster (missing/never-created, misnamed, or in a different namespace) rather than a resolver or network fault. The observable anomaly is the ~91.5% DNS-error ratio on the pod's egress path; the CONFIRMED root cause is the absence of the Redis Service/DNS record that iam-server is configured to reach. The failure is now 100% because every attempt by the Redis client to resolve the hostname fails, so all Redis-dependent requests error out. The likely trigger is a configuration/dependency mismatch (e.g., a new deployment or config referencing a Redis Service that was never provisioned, was renamed, or lives in another namespace).",
    "affected_resources": [
      "iam-server-86cb7cd948-l24zd",
      "iam-server (Deployment/Service in kylin-insights)",
      "kylin-insights-redis (missing Redis Service/DNS record)"
    ],
    "evidence_summary": "(1) Priority-1 L7 summary: pod error_ratio≈0.915 with DNS dominating the protocol distribution (91.53% DNS, 8.40% MySQL, 0.06% HTTP); all four Redis query names — kylin-insights-redis, .kylin-insights.svc.cluster.local, .svc.cluster.local, .cluster.local — show error_ratio=1.0 (100%), rps≈42.7–85.4. (2) L4 is clean: establish_fail_ratio=0, retrans_ratio=0, rst_count=0, zero_window_count=0, rtt≈0.018 ms; downstreams limited to mysql-f9db5ccd9-4d7pb + two CoreDNS pods, with NO Redis downstream. Compound diagnosis localized the fault to the egress/client path (egress error 91.58% vs ingress error 0) and explicitly excluded TCP-establish, transport-quality, zero-window and ingress-error causes. (3) Priority-3 abnormal-request samples (20/20 DNS failures over 11:12–11:27): the in-cluster names return response_code=3 (NXDOMAIN) and the bare name returns response_code=5; replies come from two distinct CoreDNS replicas (coredns-6ffd7956b8-jz7hx / -hqvsk) in 0.12–1.42 ms — i.e., fast, authoritative 'no such name' answers, not timeouts/SERVFAIL. (4) search_k8s_targets for 'kylin-insights-redis' in rd2/kylin-insights returned not_found (no matching Service/Pod), corroborating that the record/Service is absent. (5) Priority-2 could not obtain pod_uids for the CoreDNS pods (resolve_pod/search_k8s_targets not_found, inventory limited to 4 pods / 0 services), so no direct CoreDNS-side telemetry; however CoreDNS is exonerated by its fast, healthy responses in the priority-3 samples. (6) Data gaps: no trace_id on abnormal samples and 0 traces found, so no trace-level drill-down; no ready peer replicas for blast-radius comparison.",
    "investigation_summary": "Investigation progressed from symptom localization to semantic root cause across three completed priority tasks. Priority 1 (epicenter) confirmed the pod's anomaly is a DNS-layer resolution failure on the egress path and definitively ruled out L4/TCP transport faults (clean establish/retrans/RST/zero-window metrics, negligible RTT). Priority 2 attempted the resolver-side check but could not locate the CoreDNS pods in the tooling inventory, leaving an evidence gap and explicitly declining to assert either a dependency/config or a platform/DNS cause without telemetry. Priority 3 (client-side DNS semantics) closed the gap by reading the actual DNS response codes from abnormal-request samples: consistent NXDOMAIN (rcode 3) for the in-cluster Redis names answered by two healthy CoreDNS replicas in sub-2 ms, combined with an inventory search that found no 'kylin-insights-redis' Service. This convergence of (a) authoritative NXDOMAIN, (b) healthy/fast CoreDNS, (c) clean L4 transport, and (d) absent Service inventory establishes that the failure is the non-existence of the Redis Service/DNS record the pod depends on — a dependency/configuration problem — not a DNS-resolver or network fault. The remaining tasks (priority 4 dependency existence, priority 5 peer blast-radius) were effectively already covered by priority-3's service-inventory search and by the priority-1 finding that no ready peer replicas exist, so no further iteration was warranted and the root cause is reported with high confidence.",
    "detection": true,
    "localization": [
      "kylin-insights-redis"
    ]
  },
